I've written the unfamiliar-reader review to `review-unfamiliar.json` in the James Talarico run folder. It passes the review schema check, and every quoted passage matches the draft exactly. I didn't change any other file and didn't publish anything.

**Verdict:** For someone who has never heard of him, the profile works. It opens on a clear question: is his moral seriousness "the engine or the brand"? The sections build on each other, the Type 3 rival gets a full hearing, and it ends on a prediction a reader can check later. Everything I flagged is about orienting a new reader, not about the argument itself.

**Three major findings (all quick fixes):**
- **The search description says the opposite of what happened.** "Walked back into a walkout" reads as if he rejoined the walkout. In fact he left the 2021 walkout and went back to the House floor. The description also leaves out the November 3 Senate win, which is why most people are searching for him.
- **The opening never explains what the walkout was for.** The draft never says the walkout was meant to stop the House from having enough members present to pass the bill (a "quorum"). Without that, going back doesn't read as costly, and that return is the article's strongest evidence for Type 1. Readers also don't learn who he is until the fifth paragraph.
- **The 2026 race is never laid out.** The draft never says he beat Jasmine Crockett in the primary or explains who Colin Allred is. It also never says Paxton beat Cornyn for the Republican nomination. That makes the super PAC passage and the "mediocre Black man" allegation hard to judge.

**Four minor findings:**
- The intro teases "a classroom story larger than the documents," but the next classroom story readers meet is Justin, so they'll likely think that's the one meant. The 45-student claim shows up much later.
- Three phrases point back to things the reader hasn't seen yet: "the same pattern," "his aggression," and "the same Tribune report."
- Two details are left hanging: "the fines" are never explained, and "a Colbert interview CBS declined to broadcast" never says why.
- The Type 9 and Type 2 alternatives sit under the "car keys" heading, so someone skimming the headings would think they were never considered.

I marked five passages to keep, including "A failed child becomes a funding fight. A hospital bill becomes a price cap." and the closing One-versus-Three prediction.

**Similarity check:** the automatic check flagged "the three types whose core emotion is anger," which matches the Daniela Amodei draft word for word. That's a standard Enneagram definition, and the review rules say that doesn't count as reused writing, so I left it out of the findings. Rewording it would still cost nothing.

I didn't raise any new risk flags. The two already in the evidence file stay on: the single-source allegation, and the type call being only medium confidence between One and Three.
